  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts. It passes the typecheck, but I haven't run `vite build` or looked at a rendered frame, so the shaders are untested in a browser.\n\n- **Water:** the surface is a custom GLSL material (`material.custom`) on a 280×280 grid, spaced more finely near the origin and out to 200 m. The vertex shader sums four sine waves for the geometry and computes the normal analytically from their slopes. The fragment shader adds noise-based ripples that fade with distance. It also applies a Schlick fresnel mix between a deep-blue water body and the reflected sky, and a sun specular glint (a tight lobe plus a broader sheen). Far water fades into the horizon haze so the grid edge shouldn't show. A `waveClock` uniform animates it every frame.\n- **Sky:** a large inverted sphere shaded from the view direction, with a horizon-to-zenith gradient, a sun glow and a sun disc. The water reflects the same sky function. There are no textures.\n- **Camera and output:** the camera sits 3.2 m above the water looking toward the horizon. The shaders output linear colour and rely on the engine's default ACES tone mapping and sRGB encoding, which I read from the engine source but haven't confirmed visually.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()` resolves.\n\nTwo things could change once it runs in a browser. The sky sphere is scaled to 1600 and I'm not sure whether `size: 1` means radius or diameter. The camera far plane is 3000, which covers either case.\n\n",
